{
  "info": {
    "name": "Orbit Payments Mock API 2026-04-01",
    "description": "Test collection for the mock in-house payments service. Base URL defaults to http://localhost:8126. In docker-compose, the service is reachable at http://inhouse-payments-api:8126.\n\nThis is not a clone of a hosted processor. It is the shape a team ends up with when they build the ledger themselves, and four things follow.\n\n**Every movement of money is double-entry.** Captures, fees, refunds, disputes and payouts each post balanced legs across `gateway_clearing`, `merchant_revenue`, `processing_fees`, `disputed_funds` and `bank_settlement`. `/v1/ledger/trial_balance` proves debits equal credits per currency, and the available balance is *derived* from the ledger at read time rather than stored beside it.\n\n**Idempotency keys are mandatory on every write.** A POST without `Idempotency-Key` is a 400. Replaying a key returns the original response with `idempotent_replay: true`; reusing one with a different body is a 409. Only successes are recorded, so a declined card can be retried with the same key.\n\n**Payments are a state machine and it is enforced.** An illegal transition is a 409 that names the actions that *are* available. Partial captures and partial refunds carry running totals, so over-capture and over-refund are arithmetic.\n\n**The card decides the outcome.** Each stored payment method carries a behaviour, so declines, step-up authentication and capture-time failures are reproducible rather than random.\n\nAmounts are integers in the currency's minor unit and always travel with their currency. Errors are a typed envelope: `{\"error\": {\"type\", \"code\", \"message\", \"param\"}}`, and a declined card is **402** carrying the payment it created under `resource`. Requests labelled '(N expected)' assert an error path.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
